              source: "Open letter from Ben Carter, bus driver, to the Kentish Town Gazette (adapted)",
              text: "I drive the 29. I am not against bikes. I am against a drawing that treats my bus as if it were a car that had simply chosen to be long. It is not a car. It is how Nora gets to the surgery, how the school run looks when a parent cannot park, and how this street still works when the lights fail, which they do.<br/><br/>Admittedly, the air at the lights is bad. I breathe it for a living. Nevertheless, cutting twelve spaces and hoping the 29 will levitate at the corner is not a climate plan. It is a wish. Even if the lane is built tomorrow, I still have to swing out, and when I swing out I am in the oncoming path of whoever has decided the drawing is the road. Not only is it slower; it is also less safe, and I am the one who will be asked why.<br/><br/>Granted, parking is not a human right. All the same, one of those twelve spaces is the van that fills Mr Patel's shop at six, and one is the space a nurse uses when she cannot face the next stop on foot after a night shift. I am not inventing them. I see them from the cab. Whereas the consultation counts nitrogen, it does not yet count how often I am already late because a van is on the stop. That count would take a week. I would do it myself if someone gave me a pad.<br/><br/>I must admit I have no carbon-neutral timetable to offer. I have a turning circle and a duty to keep to it. Thanks to the new filters the buses are cleaner than they were, which is a fact, not a feeling. Consequently, the next honest step is not to punish the 29 for being large. It is to keep a stop and a passing place, and then to build the lane. To sum up, neither the drawing nor the street as it is will do. Both the buses and the bikes have to be in the sentence. If they are not, you have not evaluated the views. You have listed them, and listing is what we did last time, and the air did not improve. I will add what a flood does to my duty: last winter the railway went under and every extra passenger came to me. A drought will do the same in July, with bottles instead of umbrellas. Weather forecasting can name the week; it cannot make the corner wider. If the council wants carbon-neutral and a 29 that still turns, both have to be in the sentence. I am not asking for a miracle. I am asking for a passing place, and for the drawing to look at the cab before it looks at the sky. From the cab the street is not a consultation. It is a turning circle I either have or I do not. I will be at the meeting, and I will bring the pad. If they want a count of the late 29s, they can have it in a week. If they want a drawing that ignores the cab, they already have one. I know which of those two I will vote for, and it is not the one that looks tidy on a page. Tidy drawings have already had their turn on this street. The air did not improve. The 29 did not get shorter. I would like the next drawing to know both of those facts before it knows my name. That is not attitude. That is a timetable, said from the cab.",
